WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.160
Okay.

00:04.160 --> 00:08.000
So now we're going to talk about the last function in the part extraction category.

00:08.040 --> 00:10.560
We have the end of the month as the name says.

00:10.560 --> 00:14.240
It's going to go and return the last day of a month.

00:14.240 --> 00:16.080
So let's see how end of month works.

00:16.080 --> 00:16.920
This is very simple.

00:16.920 --> 00:20.080
So let's take our date 20th August 2025.

00:20.120 --> 00:23.560
If you go now and apply this function to it, what's going to happen?

00:23.560 --> 00:26.920
It's going to go and change only the day information.

00:26.920 --> 00:30.640
So instead of 20 it's going to go to the last day of the month.

00:30.640 --> 00:37.240
So it's going to go and change the 20 to 31 the last day of the month, August in 2025.

00:37.280 --> 00:41.280
Let's take another example is the 1st of February 2025.

00:41.360 --> 00:48.280
If you apply the end of the month, it's going to go and change the day from the first to 28, the last

00:48.280 --> 00:49.680
day of the month, February.

00:49.680 --> 00:51.120
So as you can see, it's very simple.

00:51.120 --> 00:55.800
Let's take another example where it is already the last day of the month.

00:55.800 --> 00:57.440
So we have 31 of March.

00:57.600 --> 01:00.920
If you apply the end of the month here, what can happen?

01:00.960 --> 01:01.800
Nothing going to happen.

01:01.800 --> 01:04.200
You're going to get in return the same value.

01:04.200 --> 01:05.320
So this is how it works.

01:05.320 --> 01:09.600
And as you can see always the output of the end of the month is going to be as well a date.

01:09.600 --> 01:11.810
So this is how end of month work.

01:11.850 --> 01:12.770
It is very simple.

01:12.810 --> 01:13.130
All right.

01:13.170 --> 01:15.890
Now quickly about the syntax of the end of the month.

01:15.930 --> 01:19.530
It can have the exact same syntax like the day, month, year.

01:19.690 --> 01:21.650
It accepts only one parameter.

01:21.650 --> 01:22.850
It is the date.

01:22.850 --> 01:27.170
So we have to pass here a date in order to find out the end of the month.

01:27.170 --> 01:30.090
So let's go and find the end of the month of our creation time.

01:30.090 --> 01:33.170
So end of the month like this.

01:33.650 --> 01:36.330
And let's have our creation time.

01:36.570 --> 01:39.570
So let's see the end of month.

01:40.250 --> 01:41.490
Let's go and execute it.

01:41.570 --> 01:44.090
Now in the output you can see we have a new column a date column.

01:44.410 --> 01:47.330
And inside it we have values about the end of the month.

01:47.330 --> 01:50.210
So for example here we have January, January January and so on.

01:50.210 --> 01:55.050
So you will see always here the end of January and the same thing for February and March.

01:55.050 --> 01:55.770
So that's it.

01:55.770 --> 01:59.090
This is a really nice function in case you need the end of the month of each date.

01:59.090 --> 02:02.410
Maybe you are creating a report or analysis where you need this information.

02:02.450 --> 02:07.450
And now you might ask me how about to get the first day of the month there like any function for it?

02:07.490 --> 02:08.370
Well, no.

02:08.570 --> 02:09.530
But there is a trick.

02:09.530 --> 02:13.930
In order to get the first day of the month using another function that we just learned.

02:13.970 --> 02:17.850
Think about it how to get the days as one everywhere.

02:17.850 --> 02:22.810
So we have to get here the 1st of January, the 1st of February and the 1st of March.

02:22.810 --> 02:23.930
So how we can do that?

02:23.970 --> 02:26.290
Well, using the data trunk.

02:26.290 --> 02:27.890
So let me show you how we can do this.

02:28.090 --> 02:33.650
So dates trunk and we can reset at the level of month.

02:33.850 --> 02:35.490
So we don't need the days.

02:35.530 --> 02:37.570
It can reset to the first.

02:37.570 --> 02:40.170
So our field is creation time.

02:40.570 --> 02:43.970
And this is going to be the start of month.

02:44.250 --> 02:45.970
So let's go and execute it.

02:45.970 --> 02:48.570
So now as you can see in the output we have the start of month.

02:48.570 --> 02:53.010
And you can see we have everywhere here a one since we reset at the level of month.

02:53.010 --> 02:55.730
And this is going to give us the first day of the month.

02:55.770 --> 02:56.850
And now you might say, you know what?

02:56.890 --> 02:58.170
Here we have a lot of zeros.

02:58.170 --> 03:00.130
How to get it exactly like the end of the month.

03:00.130 --> 03:03.810
And that's because the date trunk give us date and time always.

03:03.810 --> 03:08.930
So that means we have to change the data type and that we're going to learn later using the cast function.

03:08.930 --> 03:10.810
But we can go and do it right now.

03:10.810 --> 03:12.130
So we can say cast.

03:12.130 --> 03:14.930
And we want to change the whole thing to date.

03:14.930 --> 03:18.290
And now with that, we change the data type from date time to date.

03:18.330 --> 03:19.090
And in the output.

03:19.090 --> 03:21.170
As you can see we have only the date information.

03:21.170 --> 03:23.290
So now it's really amazing that you got two dates.

03:23.290 --> 03:26.370
The first one is the start of the month and the second is the end of the month.

03:26.370 --> 03:30.690
And those information might be helpful if you are generating reporting and you need the start and the

03:30.690 --> 03:31.410
end of the month.
